Check these items and nothing else, by reading the change: done (the change does it), explained (the Builder declined it with a reason that holds; the operator's decision after the documentation audit is not the Builder's to decline) or open (not done, or the reason does not hold). An item your last check found done or explained stays so unless the change undid it. Raise nothing new: what your first review did not ask for is not asked for now. Finish with findings copied unchanged from your first review, checks with one entry per item (id, status, note), verdict "approved" when every item is done or explained and the Builder reports the whole suite passing, otherwise "changes_requested", and comments that name what is still open, or that the suite fails.{{else}}
This is your first review of this ticket and your only chance to ask for changes: later rounds only check what you ask for now. Read the plan in docs/plans/<feature>/ (the ticket names its feature) and check, by reading, that a meaningful test exists for every acceptance test of the ticket (it checks the behaviour the ticket asks for and would fail without the change), that the code does what the ticket and the plan ask and nothing outside its slice, and that errors and edge cases are handled. List every finding at once in findings, numbered R1, R2 and so on, each specific and actionable (the file and the problem); a whole suite the Builder reports failing is a finding too. Finish with verdict "approved" and no findings when the change is correct and the Builder reports the whole suite passing, otherwise "changes_requested" with the findings, and comments that sum them up.{{end}}

Open the merge request for this ticket as a draft, with the code, using the open_merge_request tool with draft true:

{{.input.task}}

Title from the ticket; description with the builder's summary ({{.state.builder.summary}}) and a last line "How it was checked:" with the results of the earlier steps (builder's whole suite passed: {{.state.builder.tests_passed}}; code reviewer's verdict: {{.state.code_review.verdict}}). It stays a draft: the operator tests the branch by hand next, and the plan documents are added after that. If the run already has a merge request (the operator sent the work back), the tool pushes the new work onto it. If the tool result says pending_approval, end your turn without finishing: the platform resumes you with the outcome. If the action was rejected or its approval expired, finish with empty merge_request_url and branch. Finish with the merge_request_url and the branch the tool returned, never a status such as pending_approval in their place.

Audit the documents against the finished, manually tested change for this ticket:

{{.input.task}}

The builder reports: {{.state.builder.summary}}

The ticket text names its feature ("Feature: <slug>"): the plan is in docs/plans/<feature>/ in the checkout; read it. Write your requirement trace to docs/plans/<feature>/tickets/<ticket-id>.md, a new file for this ticket. That file is the only plan document you change: never edit PRD.md, ARCHITECTURE.md, TASKS.md or WAVES.md in a ticket run (tickets of one wave run in parallel and would conflict, and the platform leaves such changes out of the merge request). Write the design as built where it differs from ARCHITECTURE.md, and anything else a plan document should say after this ticket, in your ticket file under the heading "## Notes for the plan documents", one note per change, naming the document and the section; the wave's ship run applies them. You may update the project's own docs and README so they describe what the code now does (edit only documents, never code). Then trace every requirement of the PRD that this ticket covers to the code and tests that satisfy it, in that file.

If a requirement cannot be met because the PRD is silent, ambiguous or contradicts itself, or the built or required design contradicts the approved ARCHITECTURE.md or cannot work, do not decide it yourself: ask the operator with ask_operator, naming the requirement and the section, with concrete options and "Stop this ticket". Carry out the answer. When the documents are to change, write the change as a note under "## Notes for the plan documents" in your ticket file, record the decision in your trace and finish "ok". When the code is to change, finish with verdict "rework" and the decision, as precise instructions for the Builder, in notes: the ticket goes back to the Builder. Call escalate only when the operator chooses to stop.
Finish with verdict "ok" or "rework", trace_path (the path of the trace file you wrote), feature (the slug of the ticket's Feature line, exactly as written) and, for "rework", notes.

Mark the merge request of this ticket ready for review, with the code and the updated documents together, using the open_merge_request tool with draft false and paths ["docs/plans/{{.state.doc_audit.feature}}"] (the plan documents of the feature, and nothing else; of them the request carries only the ticket's own file, and the platform leaves the others out):

{{.input.task}}

Keep the title from the ticket; description with the builder's summary ({{.state.builder.summary}}), the requirements traced by the audit (trace file {{.state.doc_audit.trace_path}}) and the manual test the operator approved{{with .state.integrator_draft}}{{if .branch}} on branch {{.branch}}{{end}}{{end}}. End the description with a last line "How it was checked:" with the results of the earlier steps (builder's whole suite passed: {{.state.builder.tests_passed}}; code reviewer's verdict: {{.state.code_review.verdict}}; the operator's manual test, approved). The call pushes those documents onto the draft's branch and marks it ready; the code is already on the branch. If the tool result says pending_approval, end your turn without finishing: the platform resumes you with the outcome. If the action was rejected or its approval expired, finish with an empty merge_request_url. Finish with the merge_request_url the tool returned, and, when its answer lists left_out, those paths as left_out.
